WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.653
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:16.749
Unit twenty-six: Language Change. Today you will describe long-term language trends with the present perfect continuous.

4
00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.783
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.198
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.492
Language change.

7
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:31.410
Language change means the gradual development of a language over time.

8
00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:34.945
Language change is natural and continuous.

9
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.667
Your turn. Say: Language change.

10
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:43.068
Language change.

11
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:44.702
Borrowing.

12
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:48.448
Borrowing means taking words from another language.

13
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.025
Borrowing from French enriched English vocabulary.

14
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:55.199
Your turn. Say: Borrowing.

15
00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:58.988
Borrowing.

16
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:00.623
Loanword.

17
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:04.545
Loanword means a word adopted from another language.

18
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.242
'Café' is a loanword from French.

19
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.003
Your turn. Say: Loanword.

20
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:13.949
Loanword.

21
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:15.870
Evolution.

22
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:19.324
Evolution means gradual development.

23
00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:22.334
The evolution of spelling took centuries.

24
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.602
Your turn. Say: Evolution.

25
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:29.654
Evolution.

26
00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:31.453
Standard.

27
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.400
Standard means the variety accepted as correct for formal use.

28
00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:39.585
Standard English is used in exams.

29
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:43.068
Your turn. Say: Standard.

30
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:46.691
Standard.

31
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.219
Slang.

32
00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:52.876
Slang means very informal words used by particular groups.

33
00:01:53.483 --> 00:01:56.667
Slang changes faster than any other vocabulary.

34
00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.005
Your turn. Say: Slang.

35
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:03.937
Slang.

36
00:02:04.683 --> 00:02:05.615
Grammar.

37
00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:09.522
Grammar means the rules of a language's structure.

38
00:02:10.117 --> 00:02:12.697
Even grammar changes slowly over time.

39
00:02:13.283 --> 00:02:15.529
Your turn. Say: Grammar.

40
00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:19.368
Grammar.

41
00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:21.211
Generation.

42
00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:25.644
Generation means people born and living at about the same time.

43
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.656
Each generation brings new expressions.

44
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.995
Your turn. Say: Generation.

45
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:35.846
Generation.

46
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:40.214
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:46.222
Visal, your project traced English loanwords in Khmer. How long has this been happening?

48
00:02:47.317 --> 00:02:57.107
Khmer has been borrowing from English for decades, especially for technology: words like 'computer' and 'internet' are everywhere.

49
00:02:58.217 --> 00:02:59.671
And English itself?

50
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:10.901
English has been borrowing words for centuries, from French, Latin, Hindi and many other languages. Around a third of its vocabulary is borrowed.

51
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:15.642
Some older people complain that young people are 'destroying' the language.

52
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:25.846
People have been making that complaint for centuries. Languages that stop changing are usually languages that nobody speaks any more.

53
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.431
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.283 --> 00:03:32.630
Grammar focus: Present perfect continuous for trends.

55
00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.175
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.883 --> 00:03:37.429
English has been borrowing words for centuries.

57
00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:41.048
Khmer has been borrowing from English for decades.

58
00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.041
People have been making that complaint since the Middle Ages.

59
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:56.280
Use the present perfect continuous for a process that began in the past and is still continuing: 'has been borrowing'. Use 'for' with a period of time and 'since' with a starting point.

60
00:03:57.017 --> 00:04:01.887
Be careful! Don't say: English is borrowing words for centuries.

61
00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:06.947
Say: English has been borrowing words for centuries.

62
00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:09.932
Use present perfect continuous with 'for'.

63
00:04:10.733 --> 00:04:13.583
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:15.658
A Living Language.

65
00:04:15.958 --> 00:04:21.998
All living languages change, and English has been changing continuously since its earliest recorded forms.

66
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.980
Over the centuries, it has been absorbing vocabulary from the languages of invaders, trading partners and colonies.

67
00:04:30.967 --> 00:04:36.554
Grammar changes too, although more slowly; the pronoun 'thou', for example, gradually disappeared.

68
00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:42.364
Today, the internet has been accelerating the spread of new slang across national borders.

69
00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:49.465
Linguists generally describe these changes rather than condemning them, since change is a sign of vitality.

70
00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:54.452
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.317 --> 00:04:58.489
English blank words from other languages for centuries.

72
00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:03.394
A, has borrowed been. B, has been borrowing. C, is borrowing.

73
00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:09.432
The answer is B: has been borrowing.

74
00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:13.616
They have been studying Khmer blank 2019.

75
00:05:13.916 --> 00:05:17.175
A, since. B, for. C, from.

76
00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:22.512
The answer is A: since.

77
00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.427
A word adopted from another language is a blank.

78
00:05:26.727 --> 00:05:30.404
A, slang. B, loanword. C, standard.

79
00:05:33.967 --> 00:05:36.013
The answer is B: loanword.

80
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.242
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:39.542 --> 00:05:44.496
A, coin a new word. B, make up a coin. C, do a new word.

82
00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:50.439
The answer is A: coin a new word.

83
00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:54.070
According to the reading, linguists generally…

84
00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:58.570
A, condemn change. B, describe change. C, prevent change.

85
00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:04.423
The answer is B: describe change.

86
00:06:05.633 --> 00:06:08.971
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:09.817 --> 00:06:12.432
Which new English words have you noticed recently?

88
00:06:29.217 --> 00:06:30.647
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:30.947 --> 00:06:59.509
Recently I have noticed words like 'doomscrolling', which means endlessly reading bad news online. People have been using it more and more since the pandemic, and it captures a habit many of us share. I have also been hearing 'rizz' among teenagers, a slang word meaning charm. Some people feel such words damage English. I think, however, that they show how the language has been adapting to new experiences, just as it always has.

90
00:06:59.809 --> 00:07:01.763
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:07.857
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present perfect continuous for trends.

92
00:07:08.157 --> 00:07:13.073
Today's value is adaptability. Be ready for rain or sun and change your plans.

93
00:07:13.373 --> 00:07:16.199
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:16.499 --> 00:07:19.291
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

